    description: "**Measured**, after the cpp producer gap was closed (pure virtuals now emitted, class marked abstract):\n\n    cpp   class:Shape  (modifiers=['abstract'])\n          method:Shape::area   (modifiers=['virtual','abstract'])\n          class:Square\n          method:Square::area\n          contains  class:Shape -> method:Shape::area     <- containment works\n          extends   class:Square -> class:Shape\n          (no dispatches_to)\n\nEvery ingredient is present and the edge is still not emitted, because (`linkers/type_hierarchy.py:110` `_extends_admits_dispatch`, deny-list at :105, return at :140):\n\n    _extends_admits_dispatch(language=\"cpp\", child_kind=\"class\", child_modifiers=[])\n      -> cpp is in NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES\n      -> is_abstract_type(\"class\", []) is False\n      -> False\n\n**The rule asks whether the CHILD is abstract. For an abstract-base-class hierarchy the abstract one is the PARENT.** `class Square : public Shape` emits `extends: Square -> Shape` with `Square` concrete, so the child test can never pass — no matter how correct the producer becomes.\n\n**The rule is not simply wrong; it is right for the case it was written for.** Its own docstring names that case: *\"even in languages without virtual dispatch through concrete extends (Go, C++, Rust, C#), interface inheritance IS virtual dispatch via interface satisfaction. Go interface embedding (`type Bar interface { Foo; ... }`) and C# interface inheritance both produce extends edges between interface symbols.\"* Both endpoints are abstract there, so testing the child is equivalent to testing the parent and nobody had to choose. C++ abstract-base inheritance is the case where they differ.\n\n**Scope, and why this is filed rather than fixed in-band.** `NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES` is `{go, cpp, rust, csharp}` and the gate is shared, so changing the predicate changes dead-code reachability for four languages at once. Each needs its own ruling:\n\n* **cpp** — dispatch through an abstract base is real. But the deny-list exists because *non-virtual* cpp methods resolve statically, and the honest discriminant is per-method `virtual`, which the analyzer only now records (and only for pure virtuals). Admitting on \"parent is abstract\" is a closer approximation than today's \"never\", but it is still an approximation.\n* **csharp** — same shape: `abstract class` + `override`. Measured: `abstract class Shape { public abstract int Area(); }` / `class Square : Shape { public override int Area() ... }` emits Shape `['abstract']`, Shape.Area `['abstract']`, Square.Area `['override']`, `extends Square -> Shape`, and no `dispatches_to`. The data is there, including per-method `override`.\n* **go** — has no abstract base classes; struct embedding is composition. Not yet checked; confirm rather than assume.\n* **rust** — no inheritance at all; trait dispatch flows through `implements`, which this gate never touches.\n\n**Suggested shape.** Widen to \"admit when the child is abstract **or** the parent is\", keeping the deny-list. The caller `admitted_inheritance_edges` (`type_hierarchy.py:173`) looks up only the CHILD symbol today, so the parent must be looked up there too (the predicate's `(language, child_kind, child_modifiers)` signature cannot express it). Gate the change behind the interface-dispatch parity column so each language's cell moves visibly. Do NOT reach for per-method `virtual` tracking as a prerequisite — that is a larger producer campaign and the parent-abstract test is independently defensible.\n\n**Carries a dead-code claim if it lands**, so tag `awaits_bakeoff_validation`.\n\n**Related:** INV-tihim (the parity column that surfaced it; cpp's cell, `KNOWN_HOLES[\"cpp\"]` in `test_interface_dispatch_parity.py`, is red on THIS cause alone, its producer gap having been closed) · WI-sukav A1 (which introduced `NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES`) · audit-findings 0018 (the `is_abstract_type` predicate the rule now calls).\n\nRevised 2026-09-30 from the premise check (see discussion)."  # 1c12
- op: discuss  # 79fc
  at: '2026-10-01T17:11:19Z'  # 79fc
  by: agent  # 79fc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 79fc
  clock: 6  # 79fc
  nonce: 79fc  # 79fc
  message: "IMPLEMENTED on local branch `jgstern-agent/fix/nizug` (worktree ~/hg_wt/nizug), commit 8e1e058645. Not pushed and not merged; status is unchanged.\n\nCHANGE: `_extends_admits_dispatch` gains keyword-only `parent_kind` / `parent_modifiers`. Inside NO_VIRTUAL_EXTENDS_LANGUAGES (unchanged: go/cpp/rust/csharp) a hop is admitted when the child OR the parent is_abstract_type. `admitted_inheritance_edges` now looks up the parent symbol too. A parent with no in-tree symbol (an unresolved external base) falls back to the child-only test. The docstring's claim that Go and C# already emit interface `extends` edges was false (C# `interface IB : IA`, Go interface embedding and Rust supertraits emit no edge on dev 4dfb1d645a) and is rewritten to cite WI-ruzid / WI-gutas. KNOWN_HOLES[\"cpp\"] is removed from test_interface_dispatch_parity.py, so the strict xfail becomes a hard pass.\n\nTESTS: on the unfixed code, 8 new tests failed: the parity cell [cpp], the predicate test, the 4 abstract-parent cases, and the cpp + csharp end-to-end tests. The controls passed (concrete parent still denied in 4 languages; unresolved external parent still denied). The final targeted smart-test passed: 3897 passed, 0 failed, 4 xfailed, 126 affected files, and every changed source file is at 100% coverage. One existing test was re-fixtured: test_names_only_edges_the_dispatch_gate_admitted used a Go struct --extends--> interface edge as its \"denied\" edge, and that hop is now admitted. Its denied hop is now struct -> struct. Mutation checks: admit-all and the old child-only rule each turn the relevant test red.\n\nLIVE REPRO (run_behavior_map, worktree code): `Shape::area['virtual','abstract'] -> Square::area` is now emitted for cpp, and so are C# `Shape.Area -> Square.Area` and `Shape.Peri['virtual'] -> Square.Peri['override']`. The controls `Plain::f -> PlainKid::f` and C# `Base2.F -> Kid2.F['new']` stay absent.\n\nCORPUS (survey of dev vs the branch, fresh caches):\n- sherpa-onnx: +406 cpp dispatches_to. dead-code-maybe candidates 4178 -> 3657.\n- grpc: +1902 cpp and +3 csharp dispatches_to. dead-code-maybe candidates 17157 -> 15099.\n- powerdns: +4 cpp.\n- 0 dispatches_to removed and 0 dead-code candidates entered, in every repo.\n- Lexical check of the added edges: 11 of grpc's 1905 are false (a STATIC parent member: ConfigSelector::Equals overload, Subchannel::Create, ExternalAccountCredentials::Create, BaseNode::ChannelArgName/ChannelArgsCompare). Every other added edge has a parent declared virtual or pure virtual, or a child marked override. I did not check for same-name overloads with differing signatures.\n\nKNOWN LIMITS, filed: WI-rabut-lahih-pogiv-mugin-samaf-rurij-hokid-fitar (a concrete intermediate class denies its hop; 98 such cpp hops in grpc), WI-digaj-pafig-dulih-runuh-bovas-mituf-biriz-hogir (the gate works per hop, not per method: name-hiding/static false edges, and C# concrete-base virtual/override pairs that still get no edge).\n\nPOST-WI-kalug: unchanged. I ran the fixture with kalug's core inheritance.py + edge_types.py diff applied. The dispatches_to set was identical; the only edge change was Java IChild->IBase going from implements to extends, which the gate admits as before because Java is not in the deny-list and the child is abstract.\n\nRe-tag awaits_bakeoff_validation when this merges: it carries a dead-code claim."  # 79fc
